{
  "description": "The proposal is to remove a non-original, single-storey, L-shaped timber-framed structure at the rear of 25 Nasmyth Street, within the Bradmore Conservation Area. The structure has a pitched, single-glazed roof and timber-framed glazed doors and side windows. The small nibs of brickwork that support it will also be removed. The area will be cleared to ground level and returned to an open-air courtyard.\n\nNo load-bearing walls of the main building will be altered or demolished. The boundary walls to the neighbouring properties, No. 23 and No. 29, will be kept in full.\n\nAfter removal, the following making good will be carried out:\n\nTimber joists, wall plates, flashings and leadwork will be carefully detached from the host building.\nJoist pockets and bolt holes will be filled solid with matching brickwork in lime-based mortar.\nExposed brickwork on the host building and boundary walls will be cleaned, repaired and repointed to match the adjacent masonry in colour, texture and profile.\nExposed external walls will be made weathertight and damp-proof.\nParapets, copings and rainwater goods serving the main roof will be reinstated and connected to the existing surface water drainage.\nAny sub-floor services will be capped below ground level by certified engineers.\nThe courtyard surface will be left clean and level.\n\nThe works will be carried out in line with the submitted Demolition Method Statement, Heritage Impact Assessment and drawings 0000/A–0102/A (existing) and 1000/A–1102/A (proposed).",
